INT. COURTHOUSE CONFERENCE ROOM - DAY

A windowless room. A long table scarred by decades of folders. A water pitcher beading with sweat. Fluorescent light hums like a distant insect.

DEFENSE ATTORNEY LEONOR ASTURIAS, 40s, charcoal suit, reading glasses pushed into her hair, shuffles exhibits into a neat pile. Across from her, a PROSECUTOR, 30s, crisp, a pen behind his ear, taps a laptop. Between them, at the end of the table, sits their client MR. DESMOND TULLY, 50s, in an ill-fitting suit that smells of mothballs, hands clasped.

The prosecutor, DEPUTY DA OWEN PRATT, looks at the clock.

OWEN
We have ten minutes before the judge.

LEONOR
We have as long as it takes.

DESMOND
Can I say something?

LEONOR
(simultaneously)
No.

^OWEN
(simultaneously)
Please.

They look at each other. Desmond looks at both.

DESMOND
I was going to say I'd like some water.

Leonor pours. Her hand is steady. Owen's pen is not.

OWEN
Ms. Asturias. The offer is five years.

LEONOR
My client doesn't accept.

OWEN
It's a fair number.

LEONOR
It's a number.

DESMOND
Ten minutes, he said?

OWEN
Nine.

LEONOR
Mr. Pratt, I'd like a word with my client.

OWEN
Of course.

He doesn't move. She does not either. The pitcher drips. Desmond sips from the paper cup, studies the rim, and sets it down very carefully.

DESMOND
I didn't do it.

LEONOR
(quietly)
We know.

OWEN
(also quietly)
The evidence says otherwise.

DESMOND
Then the evidence is wrong.

OWEN
It's a very strong file, Mr. Tully.

DESMOND
My wife says you can make a file say anything.

OWEN
(a flicker of something)
Your wife testified.

DESMOND
She said what she saw.

LEONOR
She said what she believed.

OWEN
That's the same thing in a courtroom.

Leonor pulls a single sheet from the middle of her pile and slides it across the table. Owen reads it. The pen stops tapping. His color changes by degrees.

OWEN (CONT'D)
Where did you get this?

LEONOR
It was in your discovery.

OWEN
It wasn't.

LEONOR
Page four hundred and twelve.

OWEN
That's not possible.

DESMOND
(to no one)
Is it good?

LEONOR
Mr. Tully, it's very good.

Owen reaches for his laptop. His fingers hover over the keys. Outside, a bailiff's voice calls a case number, and a door slams.

OWEN
Give me a minute.

LEONOR
Take nine.
